WEBVTT

00:00.242 --> 00:01.782
- [Instructor] So we're
given this expression

00:01.782 --> 00:04.690
is the Taylor series about zero

00:04.690 --> 00:07.675
for which of the following functions

00:07.675 --> 00:09.636
and they give us some choices here.

00:09.636 --> 00:12.218
So let's just think a
little bit about this series

00:12.218 --> 00:13.323
that they gave us.

00:13.323 --> 00:14.634
So if we were to expand it out,

00:14.634 --> 00:16.761
let's see, when n is equal to zero,

00:16.761 --> 00:18.401
it'd be negative one to the zero power

00:18.401 --> 00:21.925
which is one times x to
the zero which is one

00:21.925 --> 00:23.945
over zero factorial which is one

00:23.945 --> 00:25.612
so it'll be one plus

00:26.883 --> 00:28.599
and then when n is one,

00:28.599 --> 00:30.338
well then this is going to be negative

00:30.338 --> 00:34.244
so it's going to be minus
and then x to the first

00:34.244 --> 00:36.244
over one factorial over,

00:37.136 --> 00:40.916
well, I could just write
that as x over here

00:40.916 --> 00:42.557
and then when n is two,

00:42.557 --> 00:44.679
the negative one squared,

00:44.679 --> 00:48.512
that's gonna be positive
x squared over two

00:49.385 --> 00:52.033
and there's going to
be minus x to the third

00:52.033 --> 00:54.324
over three factorial

00:54.324 --> 00:56.413
and then it's going to be
plus and I can keep going.

00:56.413 --> 00:57.804
You've seen this before.

00:57.804 --> 01:01.198
x to the fourth over four factorial

01:01.198 --> 01:02.509
and it's gonna keep going.

01:02.509 --> 01:03.892
Minus, plus.

01:03.892 --> 01:07.022
It's going to keep
alternating on and on and on.

01:07.022 --> 01:10.780
Now, our general form for
a Taylor series about zero

01:10.780 --> 01:14.095
which we could also call a
Maclaurin series would be,

01:14.095 --> 01:17.012
our general form would be f of zero

01:19.292 --> 01:21.625
plus f prime of zero times x

01:24.319 --> 01:26.486
plus f prime prime of zero

01:28.410 --> 01:30.660
times x squared over two

01:32.139 --> 01:35.609
plus the the third derivative at zero

01:35.609 --> 01:39.383
times x to the third over three factorial

01:39.383 --> 01:41.920
plus the fourth derivative,
you get the idea,

01:41.920 --> 01:46.136
evaluated at zero times x to
the fourth over four factorial

01:46.136 --> 01:48.637
and we would just go on and on and on.

01:48.637 --> 01:50.891
Now, to figure out which function,

01:50.891 --> 01:53.873
in order what I wrote in
blue to be the Maclaurin

01:53.873 --> 01:55.555
or to be the Taylor series about zero

01:55.555 --> 01:57.518
or in order to be the Maclaurin series,

01:57.518 --> 01:58.851
that means that,

01:59.970 --> 02:03.412
that means that f of zero
needs to be equal to one.

02:03.412 --> 02:06.069
It means that f prime of zero,

02:06.069 --> 02:07.744
actually let me write this down.

02:07.744 --> 02:11.222
It means that f of zero
needs to be equal to one.

02:11.222 --> 02:13.773
f of zero is equal to one.

02:13.773 --> 02:16.190
It means that f prime of zero

02:17.710 --> 02:19.592
needs to be the coefficient on the x here

02:19.592 --> 02:21.825
which is negative one

02:21.825 --> 02:22.781
and we could keep going.

02:22.781 --> 02:26.948
It means that, it means that
the second derivative at zero,

02:28.095 --> 02:29.331
well, that's going to be the coefficient

02:29.331 --> 02:31.247
on this x squared over two

02:31.247 --> 02:33.949
so that's gotta be equal to one

02:33.949 --> 02:35.356
and you see the general idea

02:35.356 --> 02:39.227
that the third derivative at
zero is equal to negative one.

02:39.227 --> 02:41.910
It's the coefficient on the x to the third

02:41.910 --> 02:45.119
over three factorial which is
negative one right over here.

02:45.119 --> 02:46.721
And so just using this information

02:46.721 --> 02:49.014
can we figure out which of these it is.

02:49.014 --> 02:51.823
So you could do a little bit
of deductive reasoning here.

02:51.823 --> 02:53.921
Let's evaluate all of
these functions at zero

02:53.921 --> 02:56.048
and see which of these are one.

02:56.048 --> 02:58.054
So sine of zero.

02:58.054 --> 02:58.953
Well, that's zero.

02:58.953 --> 03:01.127
Just by looking at this first constraint,

03:01.127 --> 03:02.474
sine of zero isn't one.

03:02.474 --> 03:03.334
We can rule that out.

03:03.334 --> 03:06.157
Cosine of zero is one so
that's still in the running.

03:06.157 --> 03:07.907
e to the zero is one.

03:09.274 --> 03:11.748
e to the zero is one

03:11.748 --> 03:15.032
and then the natural log of one plus zero,

03:15.032 --> 03:17.290
that's the natural log
of one which is a zero

03:17.290 --> 03:18.754
so that's out of the running.

03:18.754 --> 03:20.032
So just from that first constraint

03:20.032 --> 03:21.503
knowing that f of zero is equal to one,

03:21.503 --> 03:24.001
we're able to rule out two of the choices

03:24.001 --> 03:26.021
then knowing that the first derivative

03:26.021 --> 03:28.661
evaluated at zero is
going to be negative one

03:28.661 --> 03:31.239
but what's the first
derivative of cosine of x?

03:31.239 --> 03:33.574
Well, it's the negative sine of x.

03:33.574 --> 03:35.006
If we evaluate that at zero,

03:35.006 --> 03:36.300
we're not gonna get negative one.

03:36.300 --> 03:39.101
We're gonna get zero so
we can rule this out.

03:39.101 --> 03:42.045
Now, the first derivative
of e to the x is e to the x.

03:42.045 --> 03:46.012
If we evaluate that at
zero, we're gonna get one,

03:46.012 --> 03:48.632
not negative one so we can rule that out.

03:48.632 --> 03:50.557
And so not even looking at anything else,

03:50.557 --> 03:53.172
we have a pretty good sense
that D is probably our answer

03:53.172 --> 03:54.369
but we could check.

03:54.369 --> 03:58.092
The first derivative
here, f prime of x here

03:58.092 --> 04:01.409
is going to be negative
e to the negative x.

04:01.409 --> 04:04.242
So f prime of zero is going to be,

04:05.978 --> 04:09.797
is going to be negative e
to the zero or negative one

04:09.797 --> 04:11.104
so it meets that one

04:11.104 --> 04:13.235
and if you were curious,
you could keep going

04:13.235 --> 04:15.617
and see that it meets
all the other constraints

04:15.617 --> 04:17.703
but choice D is the only one

04:17.703 --> 04:20.082
that meets even the first two constraints

04:20.082 --> 04:24.249
for the function at zero and
the first derivative at zero.
